// Per-path ownership, decided at runtime.
//
// `conditional` allocates a fresh `Map` on one path and returns a parameter on
// the other. No static analysis over the call site can tell which path a
// given call took, so the callee tells the caller itself: a thread-local
// `i1` (`tyrus.return.owned`) is written at each `return`, `true` for the
// fresh allocation, `false` for the borrowed parameter, and the caller reads
// it immediately after the call, a load that trivially dominates everything
// after it since the callee's return always dominates the call site. The
// caller frees only when the bit says so, through a release kept separate
// from `ReleasePlan` (`conditionally_owned_vars`, tracked by IR bit rather
// than by static plan) so a function proven all-or-nothing owned is
// unaffected by this path.
//
// A function only qualifies when its `return`s are a genuine mix of owned and
// borrowed provenance, classified per branch of an `if`/`else` rather than by
// an end-of-function merge, an early version of the analysis shared one
// `owned` set across sibling branches and would have let an allocation named
// `x` in one branch vouch for a same-named, differently-provenanced `x` in
// another: a double free rather than a leak, caught before it was ever built.
//
// Building this found a second, real double free: reassigning a binding that
// already held a conditionally-owned value (`held = conditional(...)` a
// second time) segfaulted. The existing native-ownership reassignment path
// only releases a binding's previous value when the *new* value is itself a
// pending native-owned result; a conditionally-owned result is tracked in a
// separate pending list, so that path never saw it, never freed the old
// value, and never claimed the new one, which was then swept as an
// abandoned temporary by the same generic cleanup that frees unbound owned
// call results, while the binding's tracking entry still pointed at the old,
// now-reused bit. Fixed by giving reassignment its own conditional-ownership
// path, mirroring the native one: release whatever the binding held under its
// own recorded bit before the slot is overwritten, then claim the new
// pending result.
//
// What this does not close: a conditionally-owned result returned onward by
// a second function (an `inner = conditional(...); return inner;` wrapper)
// is invisible to the wrapper's own return analysis, since `inner` was bound
// from a call rather than a literal allocation. The wrapper is classified as
// borrowed and the caller never claims what it hands back. See
// `conditionally-owned-return-not-propagated` for that shape, measured on
// its own.
const conditional = (flag: boolean, source: Map<string, i32>): Map<string, i32> => {
    if (flag) {
        return source;
    }
    const fresh = Map<string, i32>();
    fresh.set("b", 2);
    return fresh;
};

const main = (): void => {
    const shared = Map<string, i32>();
    shared.set("a", 1);
    let total: usize = 0;
    for (let i: i32 = 0; i < 200; i += 1) {
        total += conditional(false, shared).size;
    }
    console.log(total);
    console.log("assert:", 200);
};
